        -:   36:/// (Deprecated: Use @c execute member function.) A customisation point that
        -:   37:/// executes a function on an executor.
        -:   38:/**
        -:   39: * The name <tt>execution::execute</tt> denotes a customisation point object.
        -:   40: *
        -:   41: * For some subexpressions <tt>e</tt> and <tt>f</tt>, let <tt>E</tt> be a type
        -:   42: * such that <tt>decltype((e))</tt> is <tt>E</tt> and let <tt>F</tt> be a type
        -:   43: * such that <tt>decltype((f))</tt> is <tt>F</tt>. The expression
        -:   44: * <tt>execution::execute(e, f)</tt> is ill-formed if <tt>F</tt> does not model
        -:   45: * <tt>invocable</tt>, or if <tt>E</tt> does not model either <tt>executor</tt>
        -:   46: * or <tt>sender</tt>. Otherwise, it is expression-equivalent to:
        -:   47: *
        -:   48: * @li <tt>e.execute(f)</tt>, if that expression is valid. If the function
        -:   49: *   selected does not execute the function object <tt>f</tt> on the executor
        -:   50: *   <tt>e</tt>, the program is ill-formed with no diagnostic required.
        -:   51: *
        -:   52: * @li Otherwise, <tt>execute(e, f)</tt>, if that expression is valid, with
        -:   53: *   overload resolution performed in a context that includes the declaration
        -:   54: *   <tt>void execute();</tt> and that does not include a declaration of
        -:   55: *   <tt>execution::execute</tt>. If the function selected by overload
        -:   56: *   resolution does not execute the function object <tt>f</tt> on the executor
        -:   57: *   <tt>e</tt>, the program is ill-formed with no diagnostic required.
        -:   58: */
        -:   59:inline constexpr unspecified execute = unspecified;
        -:   60:
